# Template for configs/gpu.conf, which is per-machine and gitignored.
#
# Copy this file to gpu.conf, then UNCOMMENT EXACTLY ONE profile. Everything
# here is commented on purpose: a bare copy is inert, so a fresh machine gets
# no GPU env rather than the wrong one.
#
# Which profile applies comes down to one question -- which GPU are the displays
# wired to? Check with:
#   for c in /sys/class/drm/card*-*/status; do printf '%-24s %s\n' "${c%/status}" "$(cat $c)"; done
#   hyprctl monitors | grep -E 'Monitor|scale'
# The card that owns the *connected* connectors is the one that scans out.

# --- Profile A: displays wired to an AMD/Intel iGPU (muxless laptop) ---------
# Clients must render on the iGPU that scans out. Sending them to the dGPU adds
# a cross-GPU dmabuf import per frame, which can leave windows rendering as
# garbled shrunken text after the dGPU cycles its VRAM. dGPU via prime-run.
# env = LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME, radeonsi        # Intel iGPU: iHD
# env = WLR_NO_HARDWARE_CURSORS, 1

# --- Profile B: displays wired to the NVIDIA GPU (typical desktop) ----------
# Hyprland itself runs on NVIDIA, so the NVIDIA GBM/GLX backends are the
# recommended setup and there is no cross-GPU path at all; prime-run is
# pointless here. LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=nvidia needs the nvidia-vaapi-driver
# package installed, plus MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX=1 for Firefox to reach it --
# without that package libva fails to init and video falls back to software.
# env = GBM_BACKEND, nvidia-drm
# env = __GLX_VENDOR_LIBRARY_NAME, nvidia
# env = LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME, nvidia
# env = NVD_BACKEND, direct
# env = MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX, 1
# env = WLR_NO_HARDWARE_CURSORS, 1
